#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:16
msgid "Installation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:19
msgid "`npm install js-tokens`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:28
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:31
msgid "`jsTokens`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:33
msgid "A regex with the `g` flag that matches JavaScript tokens."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:35
msgid "The regex _always_ matches, even invalid JavaScript and the empty string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:37
msgid "The next match is always directly after the previous."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:39
msgid "`var token = matchToToken(match)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:47
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Takes a `match` returned by `jsTokens.exec(string)`, and returns a "
"`{type: String, value: String}` object. The following types are "
"available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:50
msgid "string"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:51
msgid "comment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:52
msgid "regex"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:53
msgid "number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:54
msgid "name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:55
msgid "punctuator"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:56
msgid "whitespace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:57
msgid "invalid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:59
msgid ""
"Multi-line comments and strings also have a `closed` property indicating "
"if the token was closed or not (see below)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:62
msgid ""
"Comments and strings both come in several flavors. To distinguish them, "
"check if the token starts with `//`, `/*`, `'`, `\"` or `` ` ``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:65
msgid ""
"Names are ECMAScript IdentifierNames, that is, including both identifiers"
" and keywords. You may use [is-keyword-js] to tell them apart."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:68
msgid "Whitespace includes both line terminators and other whitespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:73
msgid "ECMAScript support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:76
msgid ""
"The intention is to always support the latest ECMAScript version whose "
"feature set has been finalized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:79
msgid ""
"If adding support for a newer version requires changes, a new version "
"with a major verion bump will be released."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:82
msgid "Currently, ECMAScript 2018 is supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:85
msgid "Invalid code handling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:88
msgid ""
"Unterminated strings are still matched as strings. JavaScript strings "
"cannot contain (unescaped) newlines, so unterminated strings simply end "
"at the end of the line. Unterminated template strings can contain "
"unescaped newlines, though, so they go on to the end of input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:93
msgid ""
"Unterminated multi-line comments are also still matched as comments. They"
" simply go on to the end of the input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:96
msgid ""
"Unterminated regex literals are likely matched as division and whatever "
"is inside the regex."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:99
msgid "Invalid ASCII characters have their own capturing group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:101
msgid ""
"Invalid non-ASCII characters are treated as names, to simplify the "
"matching of names (except unicode spaces which are treated as "
"whitespace). Note: See also the [ES2018](#es2018) section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:105
msgid ""
"Regex literals may contain invalid regex syntax. They are still matched "
"as regex literals. They may also contain repeated regex flags, to keep "
"the regex simple."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:109
msgid "Strings may contain invalid escape sequences."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:112
msgid "Limitations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:115
msgid ""
"Tokenizing JavaScript using regexes—in fact, _one single regex_—won’t be "
"perfect. But that’s not the point either."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:118
msgid ""
"You may compare jsTokens with [esprima] by using `esprima-compare.js`. "
"See `npm run esprima-compare`!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:123
msgid "Template string interpolation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:125
msgid ""
"Template strings are matched as single tokens, from the starting `` ` `` "
"to the ending `` ` ``, including interpolations (whose tokens are not "
"matched individually)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:129
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Matching template string interpolations requires recursive balancing of "
"`{` and `}`—something that JavaScript regexes cannot do. Only one level "
"of nesting is supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:133
msgid "Division and regex literals collision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:135
msgid "Consider this example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:144
msgid ""
"A human can easily understand that in the `number` line we’re dealing "
"with division, and in the `regex` line we’re dealing with a regex "
"literal. How come? Because humans can look at the whole code to put the "
"`/` characters in context. A JavaScript regex cannot. It only sees "
"forwards. (Well, ES2018 regexes can also look backwards. See the "
"[ES2018](#es2018) section)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:150
msgid ""
"When the `jsTokens` regex scans throught the above, it will see the "
"following at the end of both the `number` and `regex` rows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:157
msgid ""
"It is then impossible to know if that is a regex literal, or part of an "
"expression dealing with division."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:160
msgid "Here is a similar case:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:167
msgid ""
"The first line divides the `foo` variable with `2/g`. The second line "
"calls the `foo` function with the regex literal `/= 2/g`. Again, since "
"`jsTokens` only sees forwards, it cannot tell the two cases apart."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:171
msgid ""
"There are some cases where we _can_ tell division and regex literals "
"apart, though."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:174
msgid ""
"First off, we have the simple cases where there’s only one slash in the "
"line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:181
msgid ""
"Regex literals cannot contain newlines, so the above cases are correctly "
"identified as division. Things are only problematic when there are more "
"than one non-comment slash in a single line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:185
msgid "Secondly, not every character is a valid regex flag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:191
msgid ""
"The above example is also correctly identified as division, because `e` "
"is not a valid regex flag. I initially wanted to future-proof by allowing"
" `[a-zA-Z]*` (any letter) as flags, but it is not worth it since it "
"increases the amount of ambigous cases. So only the standard `g`, `m`, "
"`i`, `y` and `u` flags are allowed. This means that the above example "
"will be identified as division as long as you don’t rename the `e` "
"variable to some permutation of `gmiyus` 1 to 6 characters long."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:199
msgid "Lastly, we can look _forward_ for information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:201
msgid ""
"If the token following what looks like a regex literal is not valid after"
" a regex literal, but is valid in a division expression, then the regex "
"literal is treated as division instead. For example, a flagless regex "
"cannot be followed by a string, number or name, but all of those three "
"can be the denominator of a division."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:206
msgid ""
"Generally, if what looks like a regex literal is followed by an operator,"
" the regex literal is treated as division instead. This is because "
"regexes are seldomly used with operators (such as `+`, `*`, `&&` and "
"`==`), but division could likely be part of such an expression."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:211
msgid ""
"Please consult the regex source and the test cases for precise "
"information on when regex or division is matched (should you need to "
"know). In short, you could sum it up as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:215
msgid ""
"If the end of a statement looks like a regex literal (even if it isn’t), "
"it will be treated as one. Otherwise it should work as expected (if you "
"write sane code)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:219
msgid "ES2018"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:221
msgid "ES2018 added some nice regex improvements to the language."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:223
msgid ""
"[Unicode property escapes] should allow telling names and invalid non-"
"ASCII characters apart without blowing up the regex size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:225
msgid ""
"[Lookbehind assertions] should allow matching telling division and regex "
"literals apart in more cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:227
msgid "[Named capture groups] might simplify some things."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/js-tokens_README.md:229
msgid ""
"These things would be nice to do, but are not critical. They probably "
"have to wait until the oldest maintained Node.js LTS release supports "
"those features."
msgstr ""

